  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nMonday-Tuesday...The longwave ridge over the Northern Rockies shifts \neastward over the Mid-to-Upper Mississippi Valley as a closed \nheights center replaces it over the Northern Rockies. Diffluent\nflow aloft and strong low-level WAA should support storms\n(50-80% coverage) along the warm front in eastern Iowa, but \nlimited moisture, especially near the surface, will put a \nhamper on the coverage (20-40%) across west-central and \nnorthwest Illinois. Regardless, the southerly return flow behind\nthe warm front is forecasted to allow dew points to climb back \ninto the upper 60s and low 70s along the cold front diving \nsoutheastward as the trough over the Northern Rockies lifts \ninto south-central Canada. After another dry period Monday \nafternoon, storms will once again redevelop Monday evening \nalong the approaching cold front where a 40-knot LLJ is \nexpected to enhance the low-level shear. Current LREF guidance \nhas the sfc-to-500 mb wind shear vector oriented at a 45-degree\nangle and close to 35 knots which would suggest a mixed \nconvective mode. However, by the time any convection reaches our\nCWA, it will likely be in the form of linear clusters, \npromoting a more damaging wind threat. This is why the SPC \nhas a Marginal Risk, Level 1 out of 5, for severe storms Monday\nnight in our northwestern counties.\n\nBy Tuesday, the storms are expected to decrease in intensity\nalong the front as upper-level dynamical support is lost. A \nweak high pressure is forecasted to slide into Minnesota \nTuesday night, reinforcing the northeasterly flow over the CWA \nand pinning the stationary front in our south. This stationary \nfront will act as the focal point for showers and storms through\nthe middle of next week.\n\nWednesday-Friday...The entire area will be sandwiched between a \nridge over the Southeast and a positively tilted trough over Hudson \nBay that extends back towards the Pacific Northwest. This\npattern should support diffluent flow aloft over the residual \nstationary front as rounds of stratiform rain and storms develop\nalong it on Wednesday and Thursday. As the Southeast ridge \nretrogrades westward, the flow is forecasted to return to a more\nstable zonal flow as the boundary begins to wash out Friday \nwithout any major air mass changes. By the end of the next week \nthough, there is a good chance (50-80%) that areas across \neastern Iowa and northwest Illinois will see over 2 inches of \nrainfall with swaths of 4+ inches possible (20-30%).",
